※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4506

※ `⌈` written before one operand answers the largest of what it holds, which
※ needs it to hold several things and needs those things to be comparable with
※ each other.  A tuple whose members are of different types is neither: there
※ is no type for the answer to have, and nothing to compare a `u8` with a `str`
※ by.  Lists have the same rule and will keep it when a list may hold values of
※ several types -- what is refused is not the writing but the question.

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    let t: 〈u8, u16〉 = 〈1u8, 2u16〉
    let largest: u8 = ⌈t
    0u8
